  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nA deep upper trough stretches from the Great Lakes to Hudson Bay,\nkeeping Kansas in northwest flow aloft beneath a large ridge over\nwestern Canada. The secondary front pushed through Wednesday\nevening, and CAA is under way as a surface ridge builds south from \nthe northern Plains behind it. Wednesday reached the upper 60s, the \nthird straight day a few degrees above the NBM, as deep mixing behind\nthe morning front pulled dewpoints into the mid 30s and drove gusts \nof 30 to 35 mph. Through sunrise, most areas stay just mixed enough \nwith northerly winds above the surface to hold lows in the mid to \nupper 30s, but sheltered valleys are already decoupling. Morning lows\nwere adjusted down slightly to account for cold air drainage into \nthose low lying areas, where patchy frost is possible by sunrise.\n\nA low stratus deck over South Dakota had been a concern for this\nmorning, and while most guidance keeps the main deck north, RAP\nand HRRR soundings along Highway 36 show a saturated layer up to\naround 5000 ft, and a few REFS members bring brief low ceilings\ninto far northern Kansas around sunrise. Any clouds there should\nscatter by mid morning, leaving mostly sunny skies today with\nnorth winds gusting near 20 mph and highs from the upper 40s in\nnorth central Kansas to the mid 50s near Emporia.\n\nThe surface ridge becomes well established across the area\ntonight, and with clear skies and nearly calm winds, the night\nsets up as an efficient radiational cooling night under the\ncoldest airmass of the week. Lows in the mid to upper 20s bring a\nhard freeze Friday morning, with highs only in the upper 40s\nFriday afternoon. The ridge axis drifts east Friday night, keeping\neastern Kansas calm and clear for an even colder Saturday morning\nwith lows in the low to mid 20s, coldest in sheltered valleys once\nagain. Farther west, light south winds and high clouds return to\nnorth central Kansas on the back side of the ridge, which should\nhold lows a few degrees warmer there.\n\nOver the last several runs, guidance has cut the closed low off\nfarther south while strengthening the surface high to our north\nand east, which keeps its moisture well south of the area this\nweekend. NBM rain chances have dropped sharply over the last two\nnights as a result, and all of the ensemble clusters now hold the\nridge over the Plains into early next week. Only a member or two\nstill clips south central Kansas Sunday, so the weekend stays dry.\nStrong high pressure holds early next week before ridging builds\nover the Plains, warming highs into the mid 50s Tuesday and near\n60 by Wednesday and Thursday. The next chance for rain arrives\nlate in the week as another trough approaches, but ensemble\namounts remain small and confidence is low at that range.",
